# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/007_promotion_in_expressions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_promotion_in_expressions
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Integer promotion of a bitfield used as an operand, including the narrow-unsigned-to-int and full-width-unsigned-to-unsigned split, folded and volatile runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror.  The program
was verified to pass that gate and to run cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

Every store into a bitfield is a constant that lies inside the destination field's
representable range, so no conversion can change a value.  No compound assignment is applied to
any bitfield anywhere in the program: all arithmetic is performed on promoted operands in
expressions whose results are printed and never written back into a narrow field.  That is what
allows the strict gate to pass with no deviation while promotion is still exercised exhaustively.

No signed overflow occurs.  Every arithmetic expression is evaluated after integer promotion has
already widened its operand to int, and each result was checked against the range of int, whose
maximum is 2147483647 on every one of the four targets.  The largest magnitude any signed result
reaches is 32505856, from thirty-one shifted left by twenty; the next largest is one hundred
thousand in magnitude, from minus one thousand times one hundred; and no other signed result
exceeds 10922 in magnitude.  The only two results that wrap, the full-width field minus one and
the full-width maximum plus one, are computed in unsigned int, where wraparound is defined rather
than undefined.

The program performs exactly two shifts, and both are safe by inspection.  One is a left shift by
twenty of an operand whose promoted type is int and whose value is thirty-one, so the count is
strictly less than the width of the promoted type, the left operand is non-negative, and the
result is representable in int.  The other is a right shift by eight of an operand whose promoted
type is int and whose value is 65535, so that count is strictly in range and that left operand is
non-negative too.  Division uses only the non-zero constant divisors three and two, neither
division can be the one signed division that overflows, and no remainder operator appears at all.

No aliasing violation: no pointer is formed to any bitfield, and no type-punning occurs.  No
uninitialized read: the folded object has an explicit constant initializer for every member, the
volatile object has static storage duration and so is zero-initialized, and every one of its
fields is additionally written explicitly before it is read.  No object is modified twice between
sequence points, and no printf call receives more than one side-effecting argument.  Where a
single argument reads the same volatile field twice, which is the case only in the two squaring
expressions, no write intervenes between the two reads, so both yield the same value and the
result cannot depend on the order in which the reads occur.  No padding byte is ever observed and
no byte image is printed.  No pointer value, address, timestamp, random value or locale-dependent
format is printed.

No unsigned expression is compared against less than zero anywhere; the promoted value is printed
instead.  A comparison of that shape would be rejected by -Wtype-limits, which -Wextra enables.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared with an explicit signed int or unsigned int base type.  A plain int
bitfield's signedness is implementation-defined, so using it would manufacture a spurious
divergence; stating the signedness in the declaration removes that dependency entirely.  This
matters more in this program than anywhere else in the area, because the whole subject under test
is what type a bitfield acquires when it is promoted.

No plain char value is ever held, computed with, or printed.  Plain char signedness was measured
to differ across the four targets (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64), so
the type appears only as const char *, the element type of printf's format string and of the tag
string each reporting function forwards to it, and no char object is ever read as a number.

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) differ on
i686 (4) from the other three targets (8), so neither is printed, and no pointer value or address
appears in the output.  Every numeric printf argument is explicitly cast to int or unsigned,
except in the three nocast properties, each printed once from the folded object and once from the
volatile object, which deliberately pass a bitfield with no cast at all in order to exercise
default argument promotion at the call boundary.

Every printed value is target-invariant by derivation rather than by coincidence.  Every bitfield
is declared on an int base type, and int is four bytes on all four targets, so INT_MAX is
2147483647 and UINT_MAX is 4294967295 everywhere.  Consequently the sizeof of every promoted
bitfield expression is four on every target, a bitfield narrower than int always promotes to int,
a thirty-one-bit unsigned bitfield still promotes to int because its maximum equals INT_MAX, and a
thirty-two-bit-wide unsigned bitfield always promotes to unsigned int because int cannot represent
all of its values.  That split is the program's central assertion and it is identical on every one
of the four targets.

All four targets and all three oracles remain enabled.  Bitfield layout, size, alignment and
read-back values were measured identical on all four targets, and the promotion rules exercised
here are fully specified by the standard with no implementation-defined latitude, so a
cross-backend divergence in this program is a genuine finding rather than an implementation-defined
difference.  This program's own twelve reference cells were measured byte-identical: the reference
compiler at -O0, -O1 and -O2 for x86_64, i686, aarch64 and riscv64, executed natively or under the
target's emulator, produced the same fifty lines and exit status zero in every one.
END
